1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:05,082
Artificial intelligence is growing faster
than the factories that make its chips.

2
00:00:05,212 --> 00:00:07,700
So what happens when demand outruns
supply?

3
00:00:10,450 --> 00:00:14,566
Every robot, self-driving system, and AI
server needs silicon.

4
00:00:14,696 --> 00:00:18,964
But advanced chips are among humanity's
hardest products to manufacture.

5
00:00:20,450 --> 00:00:26,563
Enter Terafab: a proposed giant chipmaking
project backed by Tesla and SpaceX, built

6
00:00:26,413 --> 00:00:28,408
around one ambitious idea.

7
00:00:28,538 --> 00:00:29,318
Scale.

8
00:00:30,450 --> 00:00:31,664
The bet is simple.

9
00:00:31,794 --> 00:00:35,734
If computing becomes the engine of
everything, controlling more chip

10
00:00:35,584 --> 00:00:37,574
production could change the race.

11
00:00:40,450 --> 00:00:46,045
The journey begins with silicon, refined
into extremely pure crystals, then sliced

12
00:00:45,895 --> 00:00:48,568
into almost impossibly precise wafers.

13
00:00:50,450 --> 00:00:54,484
Inside a chip fab, even a tiny particle
can ruin a circuit.

14
00:00:54,614 --> 00:00:57,714
Cleanrooms protect an astonishingly
delicate process.

15
00:01:00,450 --> 00:01:03,745
Lithography transfers intricate patterns
onto silicon.

16
00:01:03,875 --> 00:01:09,150
Layer after layer, microscopic structures
begin to become real computing devices.

17
00:01:10,450 --> 00:01:15,671
A finished chip takes many repeated steps:
deposition, etching, inspection, and

18
00:01:15,521 --> 00:01:18,560
meticulous control of each microscopic
layer.

19
00:01:20,450 --> 00:01:23,185
Manufacturing is only part of the
challenge.

20
00:01:23,315 --> 00:01:28,611
Advanced packaging connects complex chips,
memory, and communication pathways into

21
00:01:28,461 --> 00:01:29,678
powerful systems.

22
00:01:30,450 --> 00:01:35,503
Terafab's vision links chip design,
manufacturing, packaging, and testing more

23
00:01:35,353 --> 00:01:36,006
closely.

24
00:01:36,136 --> 00:01:39,096
Whether it can deliver remains an open
question.

25
00:01:40,450 --> 00:01:45,462
One possible destination: advanced
computing for electric vehicles, where

26
00:01:45,312 --> 00:01:48,925
every decision depends on fast, reliable
processing.

27
00:01:50,450 --> 00:01:52,313
Another: humanoid robots.

28
00:01:52,443 --> 00:01:57,197
Real-world autonomy demands sensing,
computation, motion control, and

29
00:01:57,047 --> 00:01:58,732
split-second decisions.

30
00:02:00,450 --> 00:02:05,430
And then there's AI infrastructure: rooms
of machines running models, training

31
00:02:05,280 --> 00:02:08,031
systems, and serving millions of requests.

32
00:02:10,450 --> 00:02:12,614
The proposed scale is extraordinary.

33
00:02:12,744 --> 00:02:18,104
But scaling a semiconductor fab takes
equipment, skilled teams, reliable yields,

34
00:02:17,954 --> 00:02:19,601
and years of execution.

35
00:02:20,450 --> 00:02:22,577
Money alone won't guarantee success.

36
00:02:22,707 --> 00:02:28,371
Precision machines, energy, water, talent,
and manufacturing yield all determine what

37
00:02:28,221 --> 00:02:29,150
is possible.

38
00:02:30,450 --> 00:02:35,462
The boldest longer-term vision goes beyond
Earth: specialized chips that could

39
00:02:35,312 --> 00:02:37,644
someday support computing in orbit.

40
00:02:40,450 --> 00:02:45,256
As of late 2026, Terafab is still a
project in development.

41
00:02:45,386 --> 00:02:49,638
Its ambitious production targets are
plans, not completed milestones.

42
00:02:50,450 --> 00:02:54,544
If Terafab succeeds, it could reshape how
AI hardware is made.

43
00:02:54,674 --> 00:02:56,717
If it doesn't, the challenge remains.

44
00:02:56,847 --> 00:02:58,122
What's your prediction?

